{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-120893" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{7}{8}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005103/state.json" "file_sha256" "c03718f2375f627ef8d77f840b431c63c3bb307868569de92cdd6e2859ead4e5"} {"question" "q-120893" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{7}{8})=0.505\\,360…≈0.505\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0.505\\,4$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=0.505\\,4.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.505\\,4.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.505\\,4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005103/assets/4979d3f7031e70a642e6995c3eb819d2238281c1312a3a32c2eb4fe8dd576c24.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -0.505\\,4 \\\\  & ≈5.777\\,8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=0.505\\,4\\cdot 5.777\\,8≈2.9\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005103/state.json" "file_sha256" "c03718f2375f627ef8d77f840b431c63c3bb307868569de92cdd6e2859ead4e5"} {"question" "q-120893" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4c14e890090efcb69a6160016796e5bddeaf4ee4498b03640c924e0d467c766c"} {"question" "q-120893" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005103/state.json" "file_sha256" "c03718f2375f627ef8d77f840b431c63c3bb307868569de92cdd6e2859ead4e5"} {"question" "q-120893" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2.7"] ["math" "2.8"] ["math" "2.9"] ["math" "3.0"] ["math" "3.1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005103/state.json" "file_sha256" "c03718f2375f627ef8d77f840b431c63c3bb307868569de92cdd6e2859ead4e5"} {"question" "q-120893" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2.9"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005103/state.json" "file_sha256" "c03718f2375f627ef8d77f840b431c63c3bb307868569de92cdd6e2859ead4e5"} {"question" "q-120982" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.4$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest ten?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005103/state.json" "file_sha256" "c03718f2375f627ef8d77f840b431c63c3bb307868569de92cdd6e2859ead4e5"} {"question" "q-120982" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0.4)=66.421{…}^{∘}≈{66.42}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${66.42}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={66.42}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of \n${\\theta }_{R}={66.42}^{∘}.$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={66.42}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005103/assets/9d2b6dbe16a9537db672ddb3b369dbb49132a9f8860c774f3b8c79d478711f95.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{66.42}^{∘} \\\\  & ={293.58}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract ${360}^{∘}$ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & ={293.58}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{66.42}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=66.42\\cdot (-66.42)≈-4\\,410\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest ten." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005103/state.json" "file_sha256" "c03718f2375f627ef8d77f840b431c63c3bb307868569de92cdd6e2859ead4e5"} {"question" "q-120982" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4c14e890090efcb69a6160016796e5bddeaf4ee4498b03640c924e0d467c766c"} {"question" "q-120982" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005103/state.json" "file_sha256" "c03718f2375f627ef8d77f840b431c63c3bb307868569de92cdd6e2859ead4e5"} {"question" "q-120982" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2\\,320"] ["math" "-2\\,580"] ["math" "-3\\,440"] ["math" "-4\\,220"] ["math" "-4\\,410"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005103/state.json" "file_sha256" "c03718f2375f627ef8d77f840b431c63c3bb307868569de92cdd6e2859ead4e5"} {"question" "q-120982" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4\\,410"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005103/state.json" "file_sha256" "c03718f2375f627ef8d77f840b431c63c3bb307868569de92cdd6e2859ead4e5"} {"question" "q-121005" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the equation $\\sqrt{5}-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\sqrt{5}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005103/state.json" "file_sha256" "c03718f2375f627ef8d77f840b431c63c3bb307868569de92cdd6e2859ead4e5"} {"question" "q-121005" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ -5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ correspond to the roots of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ \n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0)={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${90}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={90}^{∘}.$\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its roots at\n\n$$\nx={90}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}\\cdot n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the other solution ${x}_{2}$ in the given range, we set $n=1,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={90}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}={270}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005103/state.json" "file_sha256" "c03718f2375f627ef8d77f840b431c63c3bb307868569de92cdd6e2859ead4e5"} {"question" "q-121005" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4c14e890090efcb69a6160016796e5bddeaf4ee4498b03640c924e0d467c766c"} {"question" "q-121005" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005103/state.json" "file_sha256" "c03718f2375f627ef8d77f840b431c63c3bb307868569de92cdd6e2859ead4e5"} {"question" "q-121005" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-{90}^{∘},\\,{90}^{∘}"] ["math" "x={180}^{∘}"] ["math" "x={45}^{∘},\\,{60}^{∘}"] ["math" "x={90}^{∘}"] ["math" "x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005103/state.json" "file_sha256" "c03718f2375f627ef8d77f840b431c63c3bb307868569de92cdd6e2859ead4e5"} {"question" "q-121005" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005103/state.json" "file_sha256" "c03718f2375f627ef8d77f840b431c63c3bb307868569de92cdd6e2859ead4e5"} {"question" "q-274587" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Arranged in **ascending order** and rounded to **the nearest integer**, the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.7$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘}$ are $x=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005103/state.json" "file_sha256" "c03718f2375f627ef8d77f840b431c63c3bb307868569de92cdd6e2859ead4e5"} {"question" "q-274587" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0.7)=(45.572\\,9…)^{∘}≈{45.573}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${45.573}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution, rounded to the nearest integer, is $x={46}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of \n${\\theta }_{R}={45.573}^{∘}.$\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={45.573}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005103/assets/3ff8bb94afda0843fa6f22802ae70ca98d7e6856e8be2911ab1cb3ea2cccd2c1.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{45.573}^{∘} \\\\  & ={314.427}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract ${360}^{∘}$ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =X-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & ={314.427}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{45.573}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈-{46}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, arranged in ascending order, the solutions are $x={-46}^{∘}$ and $x={46}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005103/state.json" "file_sha256" "c03718f2375f627ef8d77f840b431c63c3bb307868569de92cdd6e2859ead4e5"} {"question" "q-274587" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4c14e890090efcb69a6160016796e5bddeaf4ee4498b03640c924e0d467c766c"} {"question" "q-274587" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005103/state.json" "file_sha256" "c03718f2375f627ef8d77f840b431c63c3bb307868569de92cdd6e2859ead4e5"} {"question" "q-274587" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-46"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005103/state.json" "file_sha256" "c03718f2375f627ef8d77f840b431c63c3bb307868569de92cdd6e2859ead4e5"} {"question" "q-274587" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005103/state.json" "file_sha256" "c03718f2375f627ef8d77f840b431c63c3bb307868569de92cdd6e2859ead4e5"} {"question" "q-274587" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "46"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005103/state.json" "file_sha256" "c03718f2375f627ef8d77f840b431c63c3bb307868569de92cdd6e2859ead4e5"} {"question" "q-338741" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The solution to the equation $4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+9=5$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ is $x=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005103/state.json" "file_sha256" "c03718f2375f627ef8d77f840b431c63c3bb307868569de92cdd6e2859ead4e5"} {"question" "q-338741" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+9 & =5 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-4 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-1$ correspond to the values of $x$ at which $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ attains its minimum value.\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-1)={180}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${180}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={180}^{∘}.$\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find other solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its minimum values at\n\n$$\nx={180}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nHowever, since the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ is one period, and we have already found one minimum value in this period, we conclude that $x={180}^{∘}$ is the only solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005103/state.json" "file_sha256" "c03718f2375f627ef8d77f840b431c63c3bb307868569de92cdd6e2859ead4e5"} {"question" "q-338741" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4c14e890090efcb69a6160016796e5bddeaf4ee4498b03640c924e0d467c766c"} {"question" "q-338741" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005103/state.json" "file_sha256" "c03718f2375f627ef8d77f840b431c63c3bb307868569de92cdd6e2859ead4e5"} {"question" "q-338741" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "180"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005103/state.json" "file_sha256" "c03718f2375f627ef8d77f840b431c63c3bb307868569de92cdd6e2859ead4e5"}] "usage" {} "basis" 1112 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
